;; You may ignore comment {handlebars}; these are for eventual processing into an interactive tour.
;; For comment readability, you may need to turn on word wrap at ~160 characters (or window wrap and shrink your window).

#|

#####    Welcome to Lean ANSI!    #####

LANSI is broadly compatible, configured to improve most keys found on laptops while remaining fully functional with as few as 39 keys. 
Nearly all QWERTY defaults remain, and additional functionality should not interfere with current muscle memory.
It features ultra-fast latency-free typing without misfires by avoiding alpha key overloading. Death to home row mods!
It leans away from timing pressure and timing dependency for all but the most rarely used keys and features.
It prioritizes fast and accessible modifier & shortcut combos, especially from the left hand for simultaneous mousing.
It allows what would otherwise be right-handed shortcuts without leaving the mouse via mirroring (sounds harder than it is!).

Let's get into it!

{id: intro} {layer:base}
|#

;; {play-sequence: intro}




;; ===================================================
;;   TOP-LEVEL CONFIG
;; ===================================================

(include global.kbd)




;; ===================================================
;;   BASE LAYER
;; ===================================================

(deflayer base
  XX        @f1     @f2    @f3      @f4     @f5      @f6    @f7    @f8    @f9    @f10   @rf11 @rf12

  XX        @n1     @n2    @n3      @n4     @n5      @n6    @n7    @n8    @n9    @n0    @nr- @nr= bspc
  @tabultra @q      @w     @e       @r      @t       @y     @u     @i     @o     @p     enter XX   XX
  @escext   @a      @s     @d       @f      @g       @h     @j     @k     @l     @'     @;   enter
  @bspclsft @zalt   @x     @c       @v      @b       @n     @m     @,     @.alt  @/alt  @delrsft
  XX        mmid    @lctl  spc      @func-numsym

  mlft mmid mrgt @func-numsym @mirror

  @lctl    @func-numsym
)

;; If any other modifier is already pressed, space bar becomes tap-hold-press for meta.
(deflayermap (spcmeta)
  spc (tap-hold-press $tt $ht spc lmet)
  _ _
)

;; {id: base-summary} The base layer is (almost) plain QWERTY. It's easy to grow in to as the extra features should not add latency or interfere with old muscle memory. {layer: base}

;; {id: thumb-keys} Bottom row: in order to meet the goal of ANSI compatibility, only the spacebar and each key immediately to the left and right of it are utlized. Control is immediately left of the space bar and a layer key is immediately to the right. The other modifiers are set up in better places elsewhere in the layout. For maximum comfort, choose a board where the left and right alt keys are at least halfway under D and K respectively. Traditional mechanical keyboards with wide space bars will probably not be comfortable. If you need to invest in something, a split ergonomic keyboard is an upgrade in plenty of other ways, but I can *also* strongly recommend the Microsoft/Incase Sculpt or Japanese Apple Magic Keyboard for their very good thumb key positions. Using https://github.com/brandonbeaumia/touchpad-thumbkeys is also a great way to make this setup even more comfortable on laptops. {layer: base} {highlight: lctl @func-numsym spc}

;; {id: shifts} Tap left shift for Backspace and right shift for Delete. We lose the ability to hold Backspace or Delete, but this is easily made up by 1) using the now ultra-comfy Ctrl+Backspace/Delete, or 2) Using the Extend layer to *hold* an arrow and highlight backwards/forwards, then tapping backspace or delete. Backspace is duplicated in its usual much-worse spot in the top-right corner to ease transitioning.  {layer: base} {highlight: @bspclsft @delrsft}

;; {id:top-row} (Optional) The top row is not very ergonomic to reach during typing flow so it has been optimized for shortcut use. This makes it safe to overload with a Hold function. Tap these keys for their number, and hold them for Shift+Number (their symbol). Combining them with multiple modifiers is easier as you get shift "for free" when holding them. You can continue to use them for numbers and symbols, but we'll check out the dedicated NumSym layer for that shortly. {layer: base} {highlight: @n1 @n2 @n3 @n4 @n5 @n6 @n7 @n8 @n9 @n0 @nr- @nr=}

;; {id:escape-extend} The Caps Lock key is set to escape when tapped and the Extend layer when held. We'll check out that layer a bit later. {layer: base} {highlight: @escext}

;; {id: chords} We have set up just one chord/combo in this entire layout, as we must be careful to ensure responsiveness and prevent misfires when using this feature. Tapping ,. together outputs `!` and holding shift while doing so outputs `?`. Shifting this combo into ? is redundant as the `/?` key remains, but gives us the opportunity to do something very ergonomic in the future. I'll explain that option once the entire tour is over.

;; {id: base-outro} Hold the right thumb key for the NumSym layer, coming up next! {layer: base} {highlight: @func-numsym}

;; {play-sequence: base-summary, thumb-keys, shifts, alt-keys, top-row, toggle, escape-extend, base-outro}

(include base.kbd)




;; ===================================================
;;   NUMSYM LAYER
;; ===================================================

(deflayer numsym
  _         _       _      _        _       _        _      _      _      _      _      _    _

  @f12      @f1     @f2    @f3      @f4     @f5      @f6    @f7    @f8    @f9    @f10   @rf11 @rf12 _
  _         @`~     @_     @-       @{      [        ]      @}     @+±    @=≈    @|\    _      XX   XX
  _         @1      @2     @3       @4      @5       6      7      8      9      0      @:•    _
  _         @&alt   @*^    @/%      @lftP   S-4      @#     @rgtP  @,<    @.>    _      _
  _         _       _      @spcmet  _

  _    _    _    _    _

  _        _
)

;; {id: numsym-summary} The NumSym layer handles programming, symbols, and also data entry (calculator/accounting, times, dates, etc.) It's faster to use 8 fingers across home row than 3 or 4 fingers in a numpad arrangement. The symbols needed more regularly are duplicated within the layer to prevent stretching during data entry flow. {layer: numsym}

;; {id: colon} The semi-colon key is overwritten by a zero, but we often need a colon for time entry so it's moved over one slot and replaces the apostrophe (which is needed almost exclusively for text entry, not data entry). {layer: numsym} {highlight: S-;}

;; {id: numsym-transparency} No other symbols, modifiers, or control characters are overwritten from the base layer. {layer: numsym} {highlight: _} #|

;; {id: f-keys} F-keys cover the number row here for physical layouts that don't feature them (and to prevent stretching). The strange looking duplicates are for compatibility 6-column boards (Lily58, Voyager, etc): for these, F1 starts on 1 and then wraps around to F12. We will see how to access the F keys for keyboards lacking a number row in a moment. {layer: numsym} {highlight: @f1 @f2 @f3 @f4 @f5 @f6 @f7 @f8 @f9 @f10 @f11 @f12}

;; {id: numsym-personalization} This version carefully balances data entry and programming use cases and has a lot of symmetry that makes it easier to learn, but nothing is sacred here! Feel free to play around with it if you don't do a lot of data entry.

;; {id: numsym-outro} That's it for the NumSym layer! Let go of the right thumb key and you'll be back in the Base layer.

;; {id: back-to-base} {layer-base}

;; {play-sequence: numsym-summary, colon, numsym-transparency, f-keys, numsym-personalization, numsym-outro, back-to-base}

(include numsym.kbd)




;; ===================================================
;;   FKEY LAYER
;; ===================================================

(deflayermap (func)
  a @f1    s @f2    d @f3    f @f4    g @f5    h @f6    j @f7    k @f8    l @f9    ; @f10
                             v @lf11                    m @rf12
  
  ;; Modifiers lock this layer on, removing timing pressure from modifier+Fkey combos:
  lsft (multi lsft (layer-while-held func))      rsft (multi rsft (layer-while-held func))
     z (multi lalt (layer-while-held func))         / (multi ralt (layer-while-held func))
  lalt (multi lctl (layer-while-held func))       spc (multi lmet (layer-while-held func))
  
  (t! mouse-buttons)
  _ XX ;; disable other keys
)

;; {id: go-to-func} We held that right thumb key for the NumSym layer, but this time let's give it a quick TAP instead. {layer: base} {highlight: func-numsym}

;; {id: func-summary} Here we are in the function key layer. Fkeys are almost exclusively single-tap shortcuts, so this layer is set up as a "one-shot": we have a full second to tap our F key before the layer expires. Holding any modifier locks this layer on until you let go, which eliminates the timing pressure of complicated Fkey shortcuts. Alt is the most common modifier combined with F keys, so holding any of these sends Alt+Fkey. If the idea of timing pressure has you nervous, you can instead doubletap+Hold the layer key to make it stick until released. By now it's been about...20 seconds: Our layer has definitely expired! We're heading back to the base layer.

;; {id: go-to-extend} {layer: base} Now hold the Caps Lock key to go to the Extend layer. {highlight: @escext}

;; {play-sequence: go-to-func, func-summary, go-to-base, go-to-extend}





;; ===================================================
;;   EXTEND LAYER
;; ===================================================

(deflayer extend
  lrld      @ef1    @ef2   @ef3     @ef4    @ef5     @ef6   @ef7   @ef8   @ef9   @ef10  @ef11 @ef12

  lrld      XX      XX     XX       XX      XX       XX     XX     XX     XX     XX     XX   XX   _
  lrld      @eq     bspc   del      S-tab   tab      prev   vold   volu   next   pp     XX   XX   XX
  _         @accept @ealt  @elctl   @elsft  @gaming  left   down   up     rght   brdn   brup _
  _         @rec    @run   @scsht   @clipb  @caps    home   pgdn   pgup   end    ralt   _
  _         lalt    _      @lmet    @mouse

  _    _    _    _    _

  _        _
)

;; {id: extend-summary} The Extend layer's primary feature is its navigation and media clusters on the right hand, laid out in three matching HJKL rows. {layer: extend}

;; {id: extend-hrms} A super comfy set of modifiers is featured here, because your left pinky is already busy holding this layer on. Having Control, Shift, Alt and Arrow/Nav keys so easily combined with each other takes text editing to a new level anywhere Vim motions aren't supported. {layer: extend} {highlight: @elsft @elctl @ealt}

;; {id: meta} The Meta key is found on the space bar in the Extend layer. For Meta+Alpha and Meta+Num combinations, this is still a very good spot despite being one layer deep. A quick roll of Extend+Meta, then press your character. If you still need to combine it with Control you can use your right thumb for Meta and left thumb for Control. Meta is also vital to pair with arrows for a lot of window managers, so it makes sense for it to exist in the same layer. And don't forget that anything that feels too awkward using plain Meta can just be bound to Ultra/Hyper shortcuts instead (coming up shortly). {layer: extend} {highlight: @lmet}

;; {id: extend-personalization} Pretty much all the other shortcuts on the left hand here are optional, they're just things that I happen to use a lot or wanted more comfortable access to. You can look at the config file definitions for rationale. {layer: extend} {highlight: @minus, @scshot, @clipb}

;; {id: extend-outro} That's it for the Extend layer! {layer: extend}

;; {id: to-mouse-layer} Instead of letting go of caps lock, hold the right thumb key to activate the Mouse layer. You can then safely let go of caps lock. {layer: extend} {highlight: @mouse}

;; {play-sequence: extend-summary, extend-hrms, meta, extend-personalization, extend-outro, to-mouse-layer}

(include extend.kbd)


;; ===================================================
;;   MOUSE LAYER
;; ===================================================

(deflayer mouse
  XX        XX      XX     XX       XX      XX       XX     XX     XX     XX     XX     XX   XX

  _         XX      XX     XX       XX      XX       XX     XX     XX     XX     XX     XX   XX   XX
  _         XX      XX     @msup    @whlup  XX       XX     @whlup mmid   XX     XX     XX   XX   XX
  _         @whllft @mslft @msdown  @msrght @whlrgt  XX     mlft   mrgt   @slow  @fast  XX   XX
  _         lalt    XX     XX       @whldn  XX       XX     @whldn XX     XX     ralt   rsft
  XX        XX      lctl   lmet     @msnmsm

  _    _    _    _    _

  _        _
)

;; {id: mouse-summary}  The mouse layer allows you to do some quick mousing without taking your hands off the keyboard.

;; {id: wasd} Mouse directions are on the ESDF keys. WASD may be more comfortable depending on your hand size. For either option, this leaves your regular Ctrl and Shift fingers available. {layer: mouse} {highlight: @msup @mslft @msdown @msrght}

;; {id: clicks} Mouse clicks are on the right hand, lined up with a regular mouse.

;; {id: scrolling} Scrolling sideways is to the side, and scrolling up and down are on the index finger's up/down. 

;; {id: slow} Hold L to slow down the cursor for when you near your target, and ; to throw your cursor across the screen. Combining them is also a medium-slow speed. {layer: mouse} {highlight: @slow @fast}

;; {id: end-mouse} If you entered this mouse layer by tapping the layer key (right alt), you can exit it by tapping right alt again. If you entered this layer by holding right alt, just let go to return to the base layer. If you're ever unsure if you're still in this mousing layer, don't hesitate to tap right alt: there's no danger of input regardless of current mode. {layer: mouse} {highlight: @msnmsm}

;; {id: end-mouse-addendum} Tapping caps lock normally outputs Esc, but in this mouse layer the Esc is consumed by the canceling of the layer. {layer: mouse} {highlight: @msexit}

(include mouse.kbd)



;; ===================================================
;;   ULTRA LAYER
;; ===================================================

(deftemplate ultra (lkey rkey)
  $rkey (tap-hold 0 $ht (concat "C-A-M-" $rkey) (concat "C-A-M-S-" $rkey))
  $lkey
  (switch
    ((input virtual mirror?)) (concat "C-A-M-" $rkey) break
    () (concat "C-A-M-" $lkey) break
  )
)

(deflayermap (ultra)
  (t! ultra f1 f12) (t! ultra f2 f11) (t! ultra f3 f10) (t! ultra f4 f9) (t! ultra f5 f8) (t! ultra f6 f7)
  (t! ultra 1 0) (t! ultra 2 9) (t! ultra 3 8) (t! ultra 4 7) (t! ultra 5 6)
  (t! ultra q p) (t! ultra w o) (t! ultra e i) (t! ultra r u) (t! ultra t y)
  (t! ultra a ;) (t! ultra s l) (t! ultra d k) (t! ultra f j) (t! ultra g h)
  (t! ultra z /) (t! ultra x .) (t! ultra c ,) (t! ultra v m) (t! ultra b n)
  
  (t! mouse-buttons)

  _ XX ;; disable any other keys
)

;; {id: tab-ultra} The Tab key is set to tab when tapped and the Ultra modifier (Ctrl+Alt+Meta: like "hyper", get it?...) when held. This key is allows as many shortcuts as possible for workspace and app management. We don't use Hyper here because while holding Ultra, all other keys become tap for Ultra+key and Hold for Ultra+Shift+key. So Ultra+(Alpha/Num/Fkey) and Ultra+(AlphaHold,NumHold,FkeyHold) are six full sets of shortcuts that you can define in your operating system or apps. Tab is one row higher than your home row, meaning reaching your number row and even quite a few F-keys while holding it remains comfortable. {layer: base} {highlight: @tabultra}



;; ===================================================
;;   GAMING LAYER
;; ===================================================

(deflayermap (gaming)
  ;; Remove tap-hold but leave mirroring:
  (t! mirror 1 1 0 0)
  (t! mirror 2 2 9 9)
  (t! mirror 3 3 8 8)
  (t! mirror 4 4 7 7)
  (t! mirror 5 5 6 6)
  
  ;; Set most keys back to normal for proper video game holding
  esc esc ` ` tab tab lsft lsft lctl lctl lmet lmet lalt lalt ralt ralt

  ; (tap-hold 0 500 ; (layer-switch base)) 
  ;; For columnar keyboards that only have two thumb keys per side, the Gaming layer needs this workaround. You'll have to replace Caps Lock's Escape/Extend with Control within the gaming layer to have all your modifiers, but this leaves no way to get out of the gaming layer. I've left this alias here as a solution for some specific physical layouts (ZSA Voyager comes to mind), but haven't needed it myself.

  (t! mouse-buttons)
)

;; {id: go-to-gaming} To get to our last layer, hold Extend... {layer:base} {highlight: @escext}

;; {id: go-to-gaming-2} and then tap G. {layer:extend} {highlight: @gaming}

;; {id: gaming-summary} The gaming layer returns the keyboard to (nearly) standard ANSI. Besides Escape/Extend all tap-holds are reverted and all modifier keys return to their original positions. This is almost the equivalent of terminating Kanata, but leaves mouse mirroring so you can still double up your game keybinds using this feature. It's also nice for letting other people momentarily use your computer. This needs one small change for ergonomic keyboards that only have two thumb keys; you can find that note under the GAMING header near the bottom of the config. {layer: gaming}

;; {id: exit-gaming} To get out of the Gaming layer and back to Base, follow the same procedure of Extend+G, but hold it instead. This ensures that the key works the same regardless of the current state: tap always goes to Gaming, hold always leaves gaming. {layer: extend} {highlight: @gaming @escext}

;; {id: layers-outro} Those are all the layers! Two to cover all ASCII characters, and bonuses everywhere else. One final feature to cover, though... {layer: base}

;; {play-sequence: go-to-gaming, go-to-gaming-2, gaming-summary, exit-gaming, layers-outro}

;; ===================================================
;;   SPECIAL CHAR LAYERS (see include for details)
;; ===================================================

(include special-char-layers.kbd)




;; ===================================================
;;   MIRRORING
;; ===================================================

  #| ### Mirroring {id: mirroring} {layer: base} {highlight: nop0}

Mirroring really sprinkles a bit of magic into this layout and fixes so many daily keyboarding annoyances with one simple mechanic.

If nop0 (mouse back button) is held, all alphanumeric keys on the left half of the board send their *mirrored* output instead. So f becomes j, d becomes k, etc. Next time you need to Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+H, etc, give it a shot. Eventually that grows into short keywords, app launcher searches, or the first few characters of a dropdown box: it's really flowy to not have to leave the mouse for just a couple presses, no matter where they are on the keyboard. There are also some non-standard "mirrors" (tab becoming shift-tab, for example) that solve other ergonomics issues.

If you do not have a back button on your mouse, this will be completely invisible to you. I would strongly recommend investing, though! Change mfwd to nop0 if that is more comfortable on your mouse; the opposite mouse button remains open if you need a good spot to put another layer.

While using a trackpad on a laptop, the keys to the right of right alt perform the same nop0 function as the mouse back button. |#




;; ===================================================
;;   WIDE MOD
;; ===================================================

 #| ### Wide Mod {id: wide-mod} {layer: base} {highlight: @.> @/alt @delrsft}

But what about the physical layout of a board? And that thumb tucking, can we fix that? Actually, yes, with no new hardware required! One option for a laptop running Linux is setting up https://github.com/brandonbeaumia/touchpad-thumbkeys to put the thumb keys at the top of your trackpad instead of the side of the space bar. At least an 8/10 for comfort, though a little interesting just *touching* these keys while *pressing* others. For Windows or Mac, I very much like this setup as well:

Once you have gotten used to this layout (or just start with these adjustments made; I'm not your mom), we're going to make one TINY change. On the base layer, @/alt goes away. / is already in the numsym layer and ? is already available via the Shift+,. combo. Then, we move alt to the hold of our `.>` key (this does remove the "hold for >" functionality). Now we have the room to slide our hand over by one key and all those keys with it, leaving an empty column in the MIDDLE of the board instead of most of an empty column on the right side of the board. Besides losing /, this change is invisible unless you look down at the board. However, it makes right shift much more comforable by putting it below your pinky just like left shift, and it brings our right thumb over next to the right alt key as well for more comfortable layer switching.

This still leaves some thumb tucking for the left control key. There is an additional way around this problem. Using this layout day-to-day you wouldn't have noticed (unless you break typing speed records) that there is another full set of combos set up in here. If you tap space at the SAME TIME as A, S, D, or F, it results in Meta/Alt/Control/Shift (same layout as the Extend layer). Because this is a one-shot you can then release all the keys and hit your shorcut key (so for control-d you can ↓SpcD↓ ↑SpcD↑ D↓). Alternatively, if you hold at least ONE of those chord keys, the chord stays active and "pinned" by that last key (usually space bar). So for duplicating a browser tab, you could do (↓SpcD↓ D↑ [keep holding spc] L C T V). And now we have our entire layout available with no thumb tucking or pinky stretches beyond 1U of distance!
|#

;; {id: wrap-up} Wrapping up! I really hope you came away with some new ideas for your own layout, or maybe even decide to branch from this config! Thanks for looking, and toss me a star on Github if you enjoyed the read! {layer: base}

;; {play-sequence: mirroring, wide-mid, wrap-up}




;; ===================================================
;;   WINDOWS META FIX
;; ===================================================
;; Since Windows' Start menu shortcut can't be easily changed we use Kanata for it. Set PowerToys' Run (or your preferred launcher) to Win+Space to avoid the awful Start menu.

(platform (win winiov2 wintercept) (defalias lmet (tap-hold-press-timeout 0 $hold-timeout A-spc lmet lmet)))
  ;; If tapped (125ms or less), sends Alt-Space for PowerToys shortcut. Sends lmet instantaneously when used as a modifier. Sends just lmet when releasing after the 125ms expires. Using timeout variant of tap-hold-press prevents lmet spam upon timer expiration.

(platform (linux macos) (defalias lmet lmet))
  ;; Meta is left alone on Linux/Mac but still must be defined